  "summary": "Adhesive gray made from dots: skies, blood-speed and blush by pre-printed film.",
  "description": "Screentone is the craft glue of manga production: pre-printed adhesive sheets of halftone dots and patterns, cut with swivel knives and burnished onto inked pages to make skies, shadows, speed fields and texture — a physical collage process that turned black-and-white comics into richly graded worlds. Tone codes (Deleter, Letraset) became shared vocabulary; artists like Katsuhiro Otomo (Akira) and Tsutomu Nihei pushed tonal density to architectural extremes. Digital manga replaced knives with tone libraries, and the look — gradient dots, speed lines, ZIP-buyer marks — remains the visual fingerprint of the medium, even in webtoon rendering.",
